Vulnerability: KYT enable flag over-constrained (non-binary hash fed to BinaryTag)

Explanation: The enable flag is computed as `BinaryTag(ACTIVE)(isSwap ? kytDepositSignedMessageHash * (1 - isZeroDeposit.out) : (1 - isZeroDeposit.out))`. `BinaryTag(ACTIVE)` constrains its argument to be 0 or 1, but in the swap branch the argument is `kytDepositSignedMessageHash * (1 - isZeroDeposit.out)`, which equals the actual Poseidon hash of the signed message -- a non-binary field value -- whenever the hash is non-zero. So the `in * (in - 1) === 0` constraint inside `BinaryTag` fails and no proof can be produced for any legitimate non-zero signed message hash. This is an over-constraint (completeness) bug, and it repeats identically for `isKytWithdrawCheckEnabled` and `isKytInternalCheckEnabled`.

Fix: Convert the hash to a boolean before multiplying: replace `kytDepositSignedMessageHash` in the conditional with `IsNotZero()(kytDepositSignedMessageHash)` (returns 0/1 according to whether the hash is zero), so the `BinaryTag` argument is always binary. Apply the same change to `kytWithdrawSignedMessageHash` and `kytSignedMessageHash`.

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.9;

include "circomlib/comparators.circom";

template BinaryTag(active) {
    signal input in;
    signal output out;
    in * (in - 1) === 0;
    out <== in;
}

// Returns 1 if in != 0, else 0.
template IsNotZero() {
    signal input in;
    signal output out;
    component isz = IsZero();
    isz.in <== in;
    out <== 1 - isz.out;
}

template KytEnableCheckFixed(isSwap) {
    signal input kytDepositSignedMessageHash;
    signal input isZeroDepositIn;
    signal output isKytDepositCheckEnabled;

    component isZeroDeposit = IsZero();
    isZeroDeposit.in <== isZeroDepositIn;

    var ACTIVE = 1;
    component bt = BinaryTag(ACTIVE);
    if (isSwap) {
