    signal carry[CHUNK_NUMBER];
    for (var i = 0; i < CHUNK_NUMBER - 1; i++){
        if (i == 0){
            carry[i] <== in[i] / 2 ** CHUNK_SIZE;
        }
        else {
            carry[i] <== (in[i] + carry[i - 1]) / 2 ** CHUNK_SIZE;
        }
    }
    component carryRangeCheck = Num2Bits(MAX_CHUNK_SIZE + EPSILON - CHUNK_SIZE);
    carryRangeCheck.in <== carry[CHUNK_NUMBER - 2] + (1 << (MAX_CHUNK_SIZE + EPSILON - CHUNK_SIZE - 1));
    in[CHUNK_NUMBER - 1] + carry[CHUNK_NUMBER - 2] === 0;
}
```

Vulnerability: Big-integer zero check evaluated modulo the native prime is unsound

Explanation: `BigIntIsZero` is meant to assert that a big integer, represented as `CHUNK_NUMBER` limbs in `in[]`, equals zero. It accumulates carries chunk by chunk, range-checks the final carry, and finally asserts `in[CHUNK_NUMBER-1] + carry[CHUNK_NUMBER-2] === 0`. The flaw is that all of this carry accumulation and the final equality are performed in native field arithmetic, so the whole zero relation is only checked modulo the Circom prime p. Completeness still holds (a genuinely zero integer is zero mod p), but soundness is broken: a prover can supply a non-zero big integer whose limbs reconstruct to a multiple of p. That integer is non-zero as a big integer yet evaluates to 0 mod p, so the carry chain collapses and the final `=== 0` constraint is satisfied. The template therefore certifies a non-zero value as zero. Since `BigIntIsZero` underpins big-integer comparison and ECDSA verification throughout the library, this lets an attacker forge equalities (e.g. make two distinct big integers appear equal), compromising soundness.

Fix: The big-integer library must not perform the zero check over the native field. Each limb (and the accumulated carries) must be properly range-checked and the zero condition asserted limb-by-limb so it cannot wrap modulo p, or the whole library should be replaced by a mature, audited big-integer implementation. The corrected template adds an explicit per-limb range check and asserts every limb is individually zero rather than relying on a single field-reduced sum.

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.9;

include "circomlib/circuits/bitify.circom";
include "circomlib/circuits/comparators.circom";

template BigIntIsZero(CHUNK_SIZE, MAX_CHUNK_SIZE, CHUNK_NUMBER) {
    assert(CHUNK_NUMBER >= 2);
    assert(MAX_CHUNK_SIZE <= 252);

    signal input in[CHUNK_NUMBER];

    // FIX: do not let the zero relation wrap mod p. Range-check every limb so
    // it cannot encode a multiple of p, and assert each limb is individually
    // zero. A non-zero big integer can no longer alias to 0 mod p.
    component limbRange[CHUNK_NUMBER];
    component limbIsZero[CHUNK_NUMBER];
    for (var i = 0; i < CHUNK_NUMBER; i++) {
        limbRange[i] = Num2Bits(MAX_CHUNK_SIZE);
        limbRange[i].in <== in[i];

        limbIsZero[i] = IsZero();
        limbIsZero[i].in <== in[i];
        limbIsZero[i].out === 1;
    }
}
```

