Explanation: `PackBytesAndPoseidon(k)` is intended to hash an array of `k` bytes: it first runs `PackBytes` (which folds consecutive bytes with base-256 weights into a smaller set of field elements) and then Poseidon-hashes the packed result. The inputs `in[]` are never constrained to actually be bytes. Because `PackBytes` is many-to-one when its inputs exceed `[0, 2^8)`, distinct input arrays collapse to the same packed intermediate value - the report's example `[0, 1, 0]` and `[256, 0, 0]` both pack to `[256]`. Since the Poseidon hash is computed over the packed value, both inputs yield the identical hash, giving the prover a cheap second pre-image. In the registration flow the hash commits to passport / DSC certificate data, so an attacker can submit an arbitrary non-byte array that hashes to the same commitment as a legitimate document, registering arbitrary passports and DSC certificates - a soundness break.

