Vulnerability: Rotate() does not validate BigInt limbs of public-key y-coordinates

Explanation: Rotate() feeds the public-key y-coordinate limbs pubkeysBigIntY directly into SubgroupCheckG1WithValidX and into the sync-committee commitment, but never checks that each limb is a canonical n-bit value (< 2**55). SubgroupCheckG1WithValidX simply assumes well-formed BigInts. A malicious prover can therefore alter a y-coordinate to an ill-formed BigInt whose limbs exceed 2**55; this produces a commitment that honest provers cannot reproduce with canonical limbs, so no future valid proof can be generated against the stored commitment. The light client halts and user funds in the bridge are permanently locked (completeness failure).

Fix: Range-check every limb of pubkeysBigIntY with Num2Bits(n) before it is used, so any limb >= 2**55 makes the witness unsatisfiable and ill-formed BigInts are rejected.

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.3;

include "circuits/bitify.circom";
include "circuits/pairing/bls12_381_hash_to_G2.circom";

template Rotate(n, k) {
    signal input pubkeysBigIntX[512][k];
    signal input pubkeysBigIntY[512][k];

    component subgroupCheck[512];
    component yRangeCheck[512][k];
    for (var i = 0; i < 512; i++) {
        subgroupCheck[i] = SubgroupCheckG1WithValidX(n, k);
        for (var j = 0; j < k; j++) {
            // Force every y-coordinate limb to be a well-formed n-bit value.
            // Num2Bits(n) fails to satisfy its constraints if the limb is
            // >= 2**n, rejecting ill-formed BigInts before they reach the
            // subgroup check and the public-key commitment.
            yRangeCheck[i][j] = Num2Bits(n);
            yRangeCheck[i][j].in <== pubkeysBigIntY[i][j];

            subgroupCheck[i].in[0][j] <== pubkeysBigIntX[i][j];
            subgroupCheck[i].in[1][j] <== pubkeysBigIntY[i][j];
        }
    }
    // ... pubkeysBigIntY now guaranteed well-formed before commitment ...
}

