Vulnerability: I2OSP zero-padding in ExpandMessageXMD overflows the scalar field

Explanation: ExpandMessageXMD builds its SHA-256 zero-padding block by calling I2OSP(64) with in = 0. I2OSP only constrains the big-endian weighted sum of the 64 output bytes to equal in, and that equality is evaluated modulo the scalar field prime p. Because a 64-byte string spans 512 bits, which is much wider than p (~254 bits), the value in + k*p for nonzero k also fits in 64 bytes and satisfies the same constraint. A malicious prover can therefore decompose 0 as a nonzero 64-byte block congruent to 0 mod p, injecting attacker-chosen bytes into the SHA-256 input that should have been all zeros, which breaks the soundness of the hash-to-field computation.

Fix: Add assert(l < 31) inside I2OSP so the represented integer is at most 31*8 = 248 bits, strictly below p; the weighted-sum constraint then has a unique byte decomposition and the in + k*p aliasing is impossible.

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.5;

include "circuits/bitify.circom";
include "circuits/binsum.circom";
include "circuits/pairing/bigint.circom";

template I2OSP(l) {
    // Restrict the octet-string length so the represented number cannot
    // exceed the scalar field modulus p. With l < 31, the maximum value is
    // 31 * 8 = 248 bits < p, so the weighted-sum constraint has a unique
    // solution and the mod-p aliasing (in + k*p) is impossible.
    assert(l < 31);

    signal input in;
    signal output out[l];

    signal acc[l + 1];
    acc[0] <== 0;
    component byteCheck[l];
    for (var i = 0; i < l; i++) {
        out[i] <-- (in >> (8 * (l - 1 - i))) & 255;
        byteCheck[i] = Num2Bits(8);
        byteCheck[i].in <== out[i];
        acc[i + 1] <== acc[i] + out[i] * (256 ** (l - 1 - i));
    }
    acc[l] === in;
}

component main = I2OSP(64);
```

