Explanation: `EmailAuth` runs `InvitationCodeWithPrefixRegex` (pattern `( )?(c|C)ode( )?(0|1|...|9|a|b|c|d|e|f)+`) and `EmailAddrRegex` over the *same* subject and then masks with `masked_subject_bytes[i] <== subject_all[i] - removed_code[i] - removed_subject_email_addr[i]`, assuming the two matched byte ranges are disjoint. That assumption is false: for a legitimate command such as `Send 0.1 ETH to donate@codef.be`, the domain `codef.be` contains the literal `code` followed by the hex characters `f`/`b`/`e`, so the invitation-code regex spuriously matches `code...` inside the email while `EmailAddrRegex` simultaneously matches `donate@codef.be`. The overlapping bytes are subtracted twice, so `masked_subject_bytes[i]` becomes negative (a huge field element), and the subsequent `Bytes2Ints` cannot produce a valid witness. This is a completeness bug: an honest user with a perfectly well-formed command simply cannot generate a proof, making the wallet feature unusable for any email/domain whose text overlaps the invitation-code pattern.

