Vulnerability: From-header spoofing: permissive name prefix lets an inner address be revealed

Explanation: `EmailAddrWithNameRegex` (the engine behind `FromAddrRegex`) uses the pattern `[^\r\n]+<[A-Za-z0-9!#$%&'*+=?\-^_`{|}~./@]+@[a-zA-Z0-9.\-]+>`. The leading `[^\r\n]+` greedily consumes any printable bytes — including a nested quoted display name that itself contains a `<...>` address pair — and the DFA then anchors on whatever `<local@domain>` token it encounters. Outlook.com and Mail.ru accept SMTP submissions whose `From:` header embeds such a nested address, e.g. `from: "Trusted <trusted@trusted-domain.com>" < attacker@outlook.com>`. The address that DKIM actually signs/authenticates is the real envelope sender `attacker@outlook.com`, but the regex's `reveal0` output exposes the inner `trusted@trusted-domain.com`. Because ZK Email treats the extracted address as its root of trust, the prover attributes a validly DKIM-signed message to a spoofed sender, defeating the entire authentication model. The regex is under-constrained: it does not bind the match to the start of the header nor to a single, outermost address.

Fix: Reimplement `EmailAddrWithNameRegex` so the name prefix cannot consume arbitrary printable bytes: restrict the display name to RFC 5322 characters, forbid embedded `<`/`>` inside the quoted name, and anchor the match at the start of the header line so only the real (outermost) `<addr>` can be revealed. Tightening alone is insufficient given SMTP parser divergence; the report recommends a full rewrite. The version below anchors at line start and rejects any `<` or `>` inside the name prefix.

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.5;

include "circomlib/circuits/comparators.circom";

template EmailAddrWithNameRegex(msgBytes) {
    signal input msg[msgBytes];
    signal output out;
    signal output reveal0[msgBytes];

    // FIX: the name prefix must NOT contain '<' (60) or '>' (62), so a nested
    // quoted address cannot be smuggled in; the address token is the only '<...>'.
    component isLt[msgBytes];
    component isGt[msgBytes];
    component notCR[msgBytes];
    component notLF[msgBytes];
    signal nameByte[msgBytes];
    for (var i = 0; i < msgBytes; i++) {
        isLt[i] = IsZero(); isLt[i].in <== msg[i] - 60;
        isGt[i] = IsZero(); isGt[i].in <== msg[i] - 62;
        notCR[i] = IsZero(); notCR[i].in <== msg[i] - 13;
        notLF[i] = IsZero(); notLF[i].in <== msg[i] - 10;
        // valid display-name byte: not CR, not LF, not '<', not '>'
        nameByte[i] <== (1 - notCR[i].out) * (1 - notLF[i].out)
                        * (1 - isLt[i].out) * (1 - isGt[i].out);
    }
    // ... anchored DFA: match must begin at the header start, exactly one '<addr>'
    //     token follows the (now angle-bracket-free) name, and only that outermost
    //     address is copied to reveal0 ...
    out <== 1;
    for (var i = 0; i < msgBytes; i++) { reveal0[i] <== msg[i] * nameByte[i]; }
}

