Vulnerability: Start-anchor sentinel (0xff) injectable into input, enabling From spoofing

Explanation: The zk-regex compiler encodes the `^` start-of-input anchor in `(\r\n|^)from:[^\r\n]+\r\n` by prepending a reserved sentinel byte 255 (0xff) to the input and treating value 255 in *any* position as a DFA reset back to state 0 (the start state). The compiled `FromAllRegex` circuit never constrains the actual message bytes `msg[]` to differ from 255, so a malicious prover can place a raw `\xff` byte earlier in the header — e.g. inside the `Subject:` header, which Gmail and other providers accept as valid payload. That injected 0xff makes the DFA restart immediately before an attacker-controlled `from:` line, so the regex matches and reveals a spoofed sender address even though the DKIM-signed header block is unchanged. Since `FromAllRegex` is the common subroutine behind `FromAddrRegex` (used by `EmailAuth` and `EmailAuthWithBodyParsing`), the attacker produces a validly DKIM-signed email attributed to an address of their choosing. The same flaw affects every `(\r\n|^)Prefix:` regex (`ToAllRegex`, `SubjectAllRegex`, ...).

Fix: Fix the compiler so the sentinel can never collide with real input: explicitly forbid 255 from appearing in `msg[]`. Concretely, for every input byte assert `IsZero(msg[i] - 255).out === 0`, then regenerate every `common/*_regex.circom` from the patched compiler so the `^` encoding is no longer spoofable.

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.5;

include "circomlib/circuits/comparators.circom";

template FromAllRegex(msgBytes) {
    signal input msg[msgBytes];
    signal output out;
    signal output reveal0[msgBytes];

    // FIX: forbid the reserved sentinel 255 from ever appearing in the input,
    // so an attacker cannot inject a fake '^' start anchor.
    component is255[msgBytes];
    for (var i = 0; i < msgBytes; i++) {
        is255[i] = IsZero();
        is255[i].in <== msg[i] - 255;
        is255[i].out === 0;                 // msg[i] != 255 enforced
    }

    signal in[msgBytes + 1];
    in[0] <== 255;
    for (var i = 0; i < msgBytes; i++) {
        in[i + 1] <== msg[i];
    }

    component isReset[msgBytes + 1];
    for (var i = 0; i < msgBytes + 1; i++) {
        isReset[i] = IsZero();
        isReset[i].in <== in[i] - 255;      // now only in[0] can ever be 255
    }
    // ... DFA over in[] using isReset[] (elided) ...
    out <== 1;
    for (var i = 0; i < msgBytes; i++) { reveal0[i] <== msg[i]; }
}

