// Encodes a numeric date (day, month, year) into its UTF-8 YYMMDD representation
// by splitting each component into a tens digit (Decimals) and a units digit (Rest).
template DateEncoder() {
    signal input day;
    signal input month;
    signal input year;

    signal output encoded;

    signal dayDecimals;
    signal dayRest;
    signal monthDecimals;
    signal monthRest;
    signal yearDecimals;
    signal yearRest;

    // BUG: rest signals are witness-assigned and only tied by a single linear
    // constraint; they are never range-checked to lie in [0, 10).
    dayDecimals <-- day \ 10;
    dayRest <-- day % 10;
    dayDecimals * 10 + dayRest === day;

    monthDecimals <-- month \ 10;
    monthRest <-- month % 10;
    monthDecimals * 10 + monthRest === month;

    yearDecimals <-- year \ 10;
    yearRest <-- year % 10;
    yearDecimals * 10 + yearRest === year;

    // UTF-8 'YYMMDD': digits offset by 0x30 ('0') and packed into one field element.
    encoded <== (yearDecimals  + 48) * (1 << 40)
              + (yearRest      + 48) * (1 << 32)
              + (monthDecimals + 48) * (1 << 24)
              + (monthRest     + 48) * (1 << 16)
              + (dayDecimals   + 48) * (1 << 8)
              + (dayRest       + 48);
}
```

Vulnerability: DateEncoder remainder digits not range-checked to [0,10)

Explanation: Each date component is decomposed into a tens digit (`*Decimals`) and a units digit (`*Rest`) using witness-only assignments `<--`, glued together by a single linear constraint like `dayDecimals * 10 + dayRest === day`. Nothing forces `dayRest`, `monthRest`, `yearRest` into the range `[0, 10)`. The lone constraint has many solutions in the BN254 scalar field: for `day = 4` the honest witness is `{dayDecimals: 0, dayRest: 4}`, but `{dayDecimals: 1, dayRest: -6}` (with `-6` represented as `p - 6`) also satisfies `dayDecimals*10 + dayRest === 4`. A malicious prover can thus pick non-canonical digit pairs, making the packed `encoded` output - and the UTF-8 `YYMMDD` string consumed downstream - take spurious values that do not correspond to the real date, breaking soundness.

Fix: Range-check every remainder signal so only the canonical decomposition is accepted. As in fix PR #60, run each `*Rest` through `Num2Bits(4)` and a `LessThan(4)` against `10`, asserting the comparator output is `1` (`lt.out === 1`), which forces `0 <= *Rest < 10`.

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.6;

include "../../node_modules/circomlib/circuits/bitify.circom";
include "../../node_modules/circomlib/circuits/comparators.circom";

template DateEncoder() {
    signal input day;
    signal input month;
    signal input year;

    signal output encoded;

    signal dayDecimals;
    signal dayRest;
    signal monthDecimals;
    signal monthRest;
    signal yearDecimals;
    signal yearRest;

    dayDecimals <-- day \ 10;
    dayRest <-- day % 10;
    dayDecimals * 10 + dayRest === day;

    monthDecimals <-- month \ 10;
    monthRest <-- month % 10;
    monthDecimals * 10 + monthRest === month;

    yearDecimals <-- year \ 10;
    yearRest <-- year % 10;
    yearDecimals * 10 + yearRest === year;

    // Range-check each remainder to [0, 10) so only canonical digits are valid.
    component dayBits = Num2Bits(4);
    dayBits.in <== dayRest;
    component ltDay = LessThan(4);
    ltDay.in[0] <== dayRest;
    ltDay.in[1] <== 10;
    ltDay.out === 1;

    component monthBits = Num2Bits(4);
    monthBits.in <== monthRest;
    component ltMonth = LessThan(4);
    ltMonth.in[0] <== monthRest;
    ltMonth.in[1] <== 10;
    ltMonth.out === 1;

    component yearBits = Num2Bits(4);
    yearBits.in <== yearRest;
    component ltYear = LessThan(4);
    ltYear.in[0] <== yearRest;
    ltYear.in[1] <== 10;
    ltYear.out === 1;

    encoded <== (yearDecimals  + 48) * (1 << 40)
              + (yearRest      + 48) * (1 << 32)
              + (monthDecimals + 48) * (1 << 24)
              + (monthRest     + 48) * (1 << 16)
              + (dayDecimals   + 48) * (1 << 8)
              + (dayRest       + 48);
}
```

