Vulnerability: Comparator overflow — `LessThan(8)` / `GreaterEqThan(8)` inputs are not range-checked

Explanation: `depth` and `leaf_depth` are free field-element inputs that are fed straight into `LessThan(8)` and `GreaterEqThan(8)`. circomlib's `LessThan(n)` is only sound when both inputs are already bounded by `2^n`; it works by decomposing `(1 << n) + in[0] - in[1]` with `Num2Bits(n+1)` and reading the top bit. With no `Num2Bits(8)` precondition on the inputs, a prover can choose `depth` near the BN254 field prime so that the internal `Num2Bits(9)` of `depth + 2^8 - leaf_depth` still decomposes (the field wraps around) but the intended unsigned ordering does not hold. zkFuzz found `depth = p - 73`, `leaf_depth = 10` makes `exceed_depth.out = 0`, so the depth-bound check passes even though `depth` is nowhere near `< leaf_depth`. This lets a malicious prover forge a Blake3 Merkle path that claims to terminate at an arbitrary layer.

Fix: Range-check both inputs with `Num2Bits(8)` before feeding them to the comparators, restoring the comparators' documented precondition.

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.6;

include "circomlib/comparators.circom";
include "circomlib/bitify.circom";

template Blake3NovaTreePath_CheckDepth() {
    signal input depth;
    signal input leaf_depth;
    signal output is_parent;

    // Enforce both inputs fit in 8 bits so the comparators are sound.
    component n2b_depth = Num2Bits(8);
    n2b_depth.in <== depth;
    component n2b_leaf = Num2Bits(8);
    n2b_leaf.in <== leaf_depth;

    component check_parent = LessThan(8);
    check_parent.in[0] <== depth;
    check_parent.in[1] <== leaf_depth;

    component exceed_depth = GreaterEqThan(8);
    exceed_depth.in[0] <== depth;
    exceed_depth.in[1] <== leaf_depth;

    exceed_depth.out === 0;

    is_parent <== check_parent.out;
}

component main = Blake3NovaTreePath_CheckDepth();
```

