Explanation: `signal shiftedFirstByte <-- (pointAsBytes[0] >> 1);` uses the witness-only operator `<--`, which only assigns a value during witness generation and emits no constraint. Nothing ties `shiftedFirstByte` to `pointAsBytes[0]` via `<==` or `===`, so at the R1CS level it (and the output `length <== shiftedFirstByte`) is a free variable a prover can set arbitrarily. The decode loop `stringAsBytes[i] <== EmitIfInRange(5)(i, length, pointAsBytes[i + 1])` depends on this free `length`, so the extracted string no longer reflects the point's first byte. zkFuzz found a case where `pointAsBytes[0] = 1` (honest `length = 0`) yet the prover sets `shiftedFirstByte = 1` so `length = 1`, surfacing an extra `pointAsBytes[1]` byte as ballot content.

