Vulnerability: RlpU64Gadget is underconstrained when is_lt_128 is false

Explanation: The `RlpU64Gadget` encodes a U64 according to RLP rules, where a value below 128 is encoded as a single literal byte and a value of 128 or more is length-prefixed. The boolean `is_lt_128` selects between these two encodings (it drives `rlp_length`, `rlp_rlc`, etc.). The gadget only constrains the case `is_lt_128 == true`, where `cb.range_lookup(value, 128)` forces `value < 128`. There is no symmetric constraint for `is_lt_128 == false`, so a malicious prover can set `is_lt_128 = 0` while the witnessed `value` is actually below 128. This mismatched assignment still satisfies all constraints, letting the prover pick the wrong RLP encoding/length for a small value, producing an incorrect RLP serialization (e.g. of a nonce in CREATE/CREATE2) and resulting in state divergence in the zkEVM.

Fix: Add the complementary constraint: when `is_lt_128` is false, enforce that `value >= 128` (for example by range-checking `value - 128`). This binds the flag to the actual value so a mismatched witness no longer satisfies the circuit. Additionally add negative tests covering mismatched `value`/`is_lt_128` pairs.

```rust
fn construct(cb: &mut ConstraintBuilder<F>) -> Self {
    let value_rlc = cb.query_keccak_rlc();

    let is_most_significant_byte = array_init::array_init(|_| cb.query_bool());
    cb.require_boolean(
        "at most one of is_most_significant_byte is one",
        sum::expr(&is_most_significant_byte),
    );
    // ... derive most_significant_byte and most_significant_byte_is_zero ...
    let value = expr_from_bytes(&value_rlc.cells);
    cb.condition(most_significant_byte_is_zero.expr(), |cb| {
        cb.require_zero("if most significant byte is 0, value is 0", value.clone());
    });
    // ...

    let is_lt_128 = cb.query_bool();
    cb.condition(is_lt_128.expr(), |cb| {
        cb.range_lookup(value.clone(), 128);
    });
    // When is_lt_128 is false, the value MUST be >= 128, i.e. value - 128 is a
    // valid field/byte-decomposed quantity (value cannot be in [0, 128)).
    cb.condition(not::expr(is_lt_128.expr()), |cb| {
        // 0 <= value - 128, enforced by a range/byte decomposition of (value - 128).
        cb.range_lookup(value.clone() - 128.expr(), 1 << 8);
    });

    Self {
        value_rlc,
        is_most_significant_byte,
        most_significant_byte_is_zero,
        is_lt_128,
    }
}
```

