Explanation: In `assign_block_table`, the `index` column of the block table is meant to hold each block's block number, repeated across all of the block's rows, and equality constraints are supposed to (a) force all of a block's `index` cells equal to each other and (b) force that shared index equal to the `Number`-tagged value cell. The bug is that `index_cells` and `block_number_cell` are declared INSIDE the inner `for (column, value)` per-row loop. On any given row at most one column is `block_table.index`, so `index_cells` only ever contains a single element when the equality loop runs: `for i in 0..(index_cells.len() - 1)` becomes `0..0` and constrains nothing, and `block_number_cell` is only `Some` on the single `Number` row. As a result the per-block index column is never tied together nor bound to the block number across rows. A malicious prover can therefore assign inconsistent `index` values, decoupling the block table's block-number column from the real block number and producing invalid results wherever the index is used.

