Vulnerability: CallDataRLC fixed assignment is not validated against the actual calldata in the Tx circuit

Explanation: The Tx circuit lays out per-transaction fixed fields (one of which is `CallDataRLC`) and separately a calldata section containing the individual calldata bytes. For the gas cost, the circuit ties the fixed `CallDataGasCost` field to the value accumulated over the real calldata bytes (`calldata_gas_cost_acc`) through the `tx call data gas cost in TxTable` lookup. However there is no equivalent linkage for `CallDataRLC`: the `CallDataRLC` value in the fixed assignments is written but never constrained to equal the random-linear-combination of the calldata bytes in the calldata section. Because the two are disconnected, a malicious prover can put one set of calldata bytes in the calldata section (driving gas cost / length) while assigning an arbitrary, inconsistent `CallDataRLC` in the fixed field. Any consumer that trusts `CallDataRLC` as a commitment to the calldata (e.g. signature/RLP hashing) is then fed calldata that differs from what was actually used, undermining transaction integrity.

