Vulnerability: sv_address is not constrained to be constant within a transaction

Explanation: sv_address holds the address recovered by the SignVerifyChip. The circuit only constrains it on the single is_caller_address row (caller address == sv_address), but sv_address is an ordinary advice column that is never tied to be identical across the other rows of the same transaction. Because nothing forces sv_address to be constant within a transaction, a malicious prover can place the genuine caller address on the CallerAddress row to satisfy that one check, while supplying a different sv_address on the rows consumed by the signature-verification lookup. This decouples the caller identity from the ECDSA-recovered signer, allowing a transaction to be authorized by one key but attributed to a different caller, enabling unauthorized contract calls. It is a soundness bug from an incomplete translation of the intended per-transaction equality into constraints.

