Vulnerability: byte_value range check only applied to padding rows in the RLP circuit

Explanation: The "byte value check" lookup is supposed to prove every byte_value in the RLP data table lies in [0, 256) by looking it up in range256_table. Its condition, however, is q_enabled AND is_padding_in_dt, so the lookup only fires on PADDING rows - exactly the rows whose byte_value is already forced to zero. The actual data rows (where is_padding_in_dt is false) are never range-checked, so a malicious prover can place a byte_value outside [0, 256) on a real data row. Out-of-range bytes feed the RLC accumulator and downstream decoding logic, corrupting the decoded transaction while the proof still verifies - a critical soundness bug from a wrong constraint condition.

Fix: Invert the lookup condition so the range check covers data rows rather than padding rows: gate the byte_value lookup on q_enabled AND not(is_padding_in_dt), ensuring every real data byte is proven to lie within [0, 256).

