Explanation: The LtChip proves lhs < rhs by witnessing the difference as N_BYTES diff columns and enforcing lhs - rhs - expr_from_bytes(diff) + lt * range == 0 with lt boolean. The soundness of this argument depends on each diff column truly being a byte in [0, 256); the config even documents that the byte range is not checked here and delegates it to the consuming circuit. Without that range check a malicious prover can assign out-of-range field elements to the diff columns so that expr_from_bytes(diff) equals an arbitrary value, satisfying check_a while choosing lt freely. This lets the prover claim lt == 1 (or 0) regardless of the real ordering of lhs and rhs, breaking every comparison built on the chip. It is a missing-input-constraint soundness bug that any circuit reusing LtChip inherits silently.

