Vulnerability: The OneHot primitive allows more than one value at once

Explanation: BinaryColumn backs the OneHot primitive used throughout the MPT path-checking state machine. Each BinaryColumn is assumed to hold only 0 or 1, but BinaryColumn::configure never adds a booleanity constraint -- the body is a TODO that just allocates an advice column. Without a per-column Boolean constraint, the columns can take arbitrary field values, and OneHot's only check ('sum is 0 or 1') can be satisfied by non-Boolean assignments (e.g. two columns set to v and 1 - v) so that more than one variant is effectively active at once. A malicious prover can exploit this to make the one-hot encoded key/segment/path selector match multiple values simultaneously, breaking the integrity of the Merkle path-checking state machine. This is a missing-input-constraint soundness bug.

Fix: Constrain every binary column to be Boolean in BinaryColumn::configure by adding the constraint 1 - v.or(!v) == 0 (equivalently v * (1 - v) == 0), so each column can only hold 0 or 1.

