pub fn enforce_cmp(
    &self,
    other: &U128x128Var,
    ordering: std::cmp::Ordering,
) -> Result<(), SynthesisError> {
    // Collect bits from each limb, most-significant first.
    let self_bits: Vec<Boolean<Fq>> = self.to_bits_le().into_iter().rev().collect();
    let other_bits: Vec<Boolean<Fq>> = other.to_bits_le().into_iter().rev().collect();

    // Single accumulator: starts true and stays true until a "stop" bit.
    let mut acc: Boolean<Fq> = Boolean::constant(true);
    for (self_bit, other_bit) in zip(self_bits, other_bits) {
        match ordering {
            std::cmp::Ordering::Equal => unimplemented!("use `EqGadget` instead"),
            std::cmp::Ordering::Less => {
                // this_bit_eq = self \/ !other  (false only when self=0, other=1)
                let this_bit_eq = self_bit.or(&other_bit.not())?;
                acc = acc.and(&this_bit_eq)?;
            }
            std::cmp::Ordering::Greater => {
                // this_bit_eq = !self \/ other  (false only when self=1, other=0)
                let this_bit_eq = (self_bit.not()).or(&other_bit)?;
                acc = acc.and(&this_bit_eq)?;
            }
        }
    }
    // Require that some "stop" bit fired, i.e. acc became false.
    acc.enforce_equal(&Boolean::constant(false))?;
    Ok(())
}
```

Vulnerability: Invalid comparisons on fixed-point values are accepted by the circuit verifier

Explanation: `enforce_cmp` walks the 256-bit representations of `self` and `other` from the most significant bit and folds a single boolean `acc` with `and`, then requires `acc == false`. The intent is that `acc` stays true while the operands are equal and flips to false at the first bit that proves the required ordering. But a single two-state accumulator cannot distinguish 'still equal so far' from 'ordering already decided'. Once any lower, less-significant bit makes `this_bit_eq` false, `acc` becomes false and the check passes, even if a more significant bit already established the opposite ordering. The comparison therefore does not stop at the first real discrepancy, so the circuit accepts invalid pairs (e.g. asserting `a < b` when actually `a > b`). Any mechanism that relies on these fixed-point comparisons for correctness is de-secured.

Fix: Replace the single accumulator with proper ternary state by tracking two booleans, `gt` (greater-than) and `lt` (less-than), updating them only at the most significant differing bit so the comparison stops at the first real discrepancy, and then enforce the requested ordering. Add unit tests that feed invalid inequalities and assert the prover refuses to create a proof.

```rust
pub fn enforce_cmp(
    &self,
    other: &U128x128Var,
    ordering: std::cmp::Ordering,
) -> Result<(), SynthesisError> {
    let self_bits: Vec<Boolean<Fq>> = self.to_bits_le().into_iter().rev().collect();
    let other_bits: Vec<Boolean<Fq>> = other.to_bits_le().into_iter().rev().collect();

    // Ternary state: `gt`/`lt` record the decision at the first differing bit;
    // `decided` freezes them so less-significant bits cannot override it.
    let mut gt = Boolean::constant(false);
    let mut lt = Boolean::constant(false);
    let mut decided = Boolean::constant(false);

    for (self_bit, other_bit) in zip(self_bits, other_bits) {
        // This bit differs and we have not decided yet.
        let bits_differ = self_bit.xor(&other_bit)?;
        let first_diff = bits_differ.and(&decided.not())?;

        // self=1, other=0 at the first differing bit => greater.
        let this_gt = first_diff.and(&self_bit)?;
        // self=0, other=1 at the first differing bit => less.
        let this_lt = first_diff.and(&other_bit)?;

        gt = gt.or(&this_gt)?;
        lt = lt.or(&this_lt)?;
        decided = decided.or(&bits_differ)?;
    }

    match ordering {
        std::cmp::Ordering::Equal => unimplemented!("use `EqGadget` instead"),
        std::cmp::Ordering::Less => lt.enforce_equal(&Boolean::constant(true))?,
        std::cmp::Ordering::Greater => gt.enforce_equal(&Boolean::constant(true))?,
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

