Vulnerability: Under-constrained is_complete flag lets partial execution pass as complete

Explanation: This is the first-layer recursive verifier in SP1. `is_complete` is a boolean public value asserting that the aggregated shard proofs constitute a *complete* program execution (start pc / shard at the genesis values, end pc halted, single contiguous shard range, etc.). Here `is_complete` is taken directly from the prover-controlled witness and written into `recursion_public_values.is_complete` without any constraint linking it to the aggregated state. There is no call to `assert_complete`, so nothing forces `is_complete == 1` to imply the execution actually ran to completion. A malicious prover can set `is_complete = 1` on a proof of partial execution; downstream verifiers (compress/shrink/wrap and on-chain) trust this flag, so a partial-execution proof is accepted as a complete one.

Fix: Constrain the flag with `assert_complete` in every part of the recursive verifier (including the first recursion layer): when `is_complete` is set it must enforce the completeness conditions on the aggregated public values (start state, halted next_pc, cumulative sum, single shard range, etc.). Implemented in SP1 v4.0.0.

```rust
pub fn verify(
    builder: &mut Builder<C>,
    machine: &StarkMachine<SC, RiscvAir<SC::Val>>,
    input: SP1RecursionWitnessVariable<C, SC>,
) {
    let SP1RecursionWitnessVariable {
        vk, shard_proofs, leaf_challenger, initial_reconstruct_challenger,
        is_complete, is_first_shard, vk_root,
    } = input;

    // ... verify each shard proof and aggregate public values ...

    let mut recursion_public_values_stream = [zero; RECURSIVE_PROOF_NUM_PV_ELTS];
    let recursion_public_values: &mut RecursionPublicValues<_> =
        recursion_public_values_stream.as_mut_slice().borrow_mut();
    recursion_public_values.start_pc = start_pc;
    recursion_public_values.next_pc = current_pc;
    // ... fill all the other aggregated public values ...
    recursion_public_values.exit_code = exit_code;
    recursion_public_values.is_complete = is_complete;
    recursion_public_values.vk_root = vk_root;

    // Constrain that `is_complete` truly reflects a complete execution.
    // When is_complete == 1 this enforces the genesis/halt/cumulative-sum conditions.
    assert_complete::<C, SC>(builder, recursion_public_values, is_complete);

    recursion_public_values.digest =
        recursion_public_values_digest::<C, SC>(builder, recursion_public_values);
    SC::commit_recursion_public_values(builder, *recursion_public_values);
}
