Vulnerability: Underconstrained memory when rs1 == rs2 in 3-register instructions

Explanation: This is the rv32im execution circuit for 3-register RISC-V instructions (e.g. `remu`, `divu`, and the ALU ops in `step_compute`/`step_store`). Each such instruction performs two register reads, `load_register(rs1)` and `load_register(rs2)`, within a single memory cycle. When `rs1 == rs2`, the circuit issues two reads to the *same* memory address inside one cycle, but the memory subsystem's consistency argument lacks a constraint to handle multiple accesses to the same address in the same cycle. This leaves the second read under-constrained: a malicious prover can make `rs1` and `rs2` take inconsistent values for the same register in one cycle, so e.g. `divu`/`remu` with `rs1 == rs2` yields an attacker-chosen result that still satisfies the circuit, breaking soundness and allowing forged execution proofs.

Fix: Constrain the memory argument so that when two register reads in one cycle target the same address they are forced to read the same value (circuit fix in zirgen PR #238 / risc0 PR #3181). Upgrade to risc0-zkvm >= 2.1.0 (risc0-circuit-rv32im >= 2.0.4). The execution model below mirrors the fixed circuit by reading the shared register once when rs1 == rs2.

```rust
fn step_compute<M: EmuContext>(
    &mut self,
    ctx: &mut M,
    kind: InsnKind,
    decoded: DecodedInstruction,
) -> Result<Option<InsnKind>> {
    self.trace_instruction(ctx, kind, &decoded)?;

    let pc = ctx.get_pc();
    let mut new_pc = pc + WORD_SIZE;
    let mut rd = decoded.rd;
    // The circuit now constrains same-address reads within a cycle to be equal,
    // so the two operands of a 3-register instruction are consistent even when
    // rs1 == rs2.
    let rs1 = ctx.load_register(decoded.rs1 as usize)?;
    let rs2 = if decoded.rs1 == decoded.rs2 {
        rs1
    } else {
        ctx.load_register(decoded.rs2 as usize)?
    };
    let imm_i = decoded.imm_i();
    let out = match kind {
        InsnKind::Add => rs1.wrapping_add(rs2),
        InsnKind::Sub => rs1.wrapping_sub(rs2),
        InsnKind::Sll => rs1 << (rs2 & 0x1f),
        // ... remu, divu and all other 3-register ALU ops ...
        _ => unreachable!(),
    };
    // ... write rd, advance pc ...
    Ok(None)
}
```

