Vulnerability: Underconstrained division: two valid outputs and unconstrained div-by-zero quotient

Explanation: This is the rv32im DoDiv gadget (signed/unsigned integer division), identified by Veridise's Picus tool. The quotient and remainder come from an untrusted extern hint and are pinned only by `numerator == quot*denom + rem` plus a remainder-magnitude check. Two soundness gaps exist. (1) For some signed inputs the `denominator != 0` branch's sign/magnitude relation (`|rem| < |denom|` via `CmpLessThanUnsigned`) is satisfied by *two* distinct `(quot, rem)` pairs, so the circuit accepts a quotient that is off by one with a compensating remainder, i.e. division has two valid outputs where only one is the correct RISC-V result. (2) In the `denominator == 0` branch (`x23` is true) only `rem == numerator` is asserted; the quotient `x11` is left completely unconstrained, so a malicious prover can make `divu`/`div` by zero return any value (RISC-V mandates the all-ones result). Either gap lets a prover produce an execution proof with an attacker-chosen division result.

Fix: Fully constrain both branches: in the divisor-zero branch pin the quotient to the RISC-V specified value (all-ones, 0xFFFFFFFF) instead of leaving it free, and tighten the non-zero branch so the (quotient, remainder) pair is unique (correct sign of the remainder and strict `|rem| < |denom|`). Fixed in zirgen issue #249 / risc0 PR #3235; upgrade to risc0-zkvm >= 2.2.0 (risc0-circuit-rv32im >= 3.0.0).

