Explanation: left_child_jump_until_inside_mmr decides whether left_child is inside the MMR using the prover-supplied hint in_mmr, and each branch only asserts one difference into a range-check cell: mmr_len - left_child (claiming left_child <= mmr_len) or left_child - mmr_len - 1 (claiming mmr_len < left_child). The range-check builtin merely proves the asserted value is in [0, 2^128); it is equivalent to the intended comparison only when mmr_len is a bounded non-negative integer. Because mmr_len is passed in as an implicit argument and never range checked, a malicious prover can choose a field element for mmr_len for which mmr_len - left_child wraps into the valid range-check window, satisfying the 'inside' branch assertion for a left_child that is not actually inside the tree (or vice versa). This makes the termination/position logic of the peak computation unsound, letting the prover steer which positions are treated as peaks and forge the root.

Fix: Range check mmr_len (assert it is a non-negative bounded integer) before performing the comparisons.

```cairo
func left_child_jump_until_inside_mmr{range_check_ptr, pow2_array: felt*, mmr_len}(
    left_child: felt
) -> felt {
    alloc_locals;
    // Range check mmr_len so the comparisons below soundly reflect the intended inequalities.
    assert_nn(mmr_len);
    local in_mmr;

    %{ ids.in_mmr = 1 if ids.left_child<=ids.mmr_len else 0 %}
    if (in_mmr != 0) {
        // Ensure left_child <= mmr_len
        assert [range_check_ptr] = mmr_len - left_child;
        tempvar range_check_ptr = range_check_ptr + 1;
        return left_child;
    } else {
        // Ensure mmr_len < left_child
        assert [range_check_ptr] = left_child - mmr_len - 1;
        tempvar range_check_ptr = range_check_ptr + 1;

        let height = compute_height_pre_alloc_pow2(left_child);
        let left_child = left_child - pow2_array[height];
        return left_child_jump_until_inside_mmr(left_child);
    }
}
```

