Vulnerability: Behaviour is undefined if peaks_len is not range checked

Explanation: peaks_len counts the MMR peaks and is used both as a write index (peaks[peaks_len]) and to seed the recursion get_peaks_from_positions_inner(..., peaks_len - 1). It is never range checked to be a small non-negative integer. Over the field, peaks_len - 1 with peaks_len == 0 underflows to p - 1 (a huge value), so the index argument passed to the inner recursion is not the intended decrementing counter and the base case index == 0 is reached only after an astronomically long / undefined recursion, reading and writing arbitrary memory. Likewise an attacker-influenced peaks_len lets peaks[peaks_len] write past the intended peaks buffer. Because the number of peaks is a deterministic function of mmr_len, accepting an unconstrained peaks_len lets a malicious prover process the wrong number of peaks and bag a forged root, breaking soundness.

Fix: Range check peaks_len so it is a bounded, non-negative integer (and at least 1 before computing peaks_len - 1) before using it as a counter/index.

```cairo
func compute_peaks_inner{range_check_ptr, pow2_array: felt*, mmr_len: felt}(
    peaks: felt*, peaks_len: felt, mmr_pos: felt
) -> felt {
    alloc_locals;
    if (mmr_pos == mmr_len) {
        return peaks_len;
    } else {
        let height = compute_height_pre_alloc_pow2(mmr_pos);
        let right_sibling = mmr_pos + pow2_array[height + 1] - 1;
        let left_child = left_child_jump_until_inside_mmr(right_sibling);
        assert peaks[peaks_len] = left_child;
        return compute_peaks_inner(peaks, peaks_len + 1, left_child);
    }
}

